  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nWe propose to replace the local optimization method with a randomized global optimization algorithm inspired by simulated annealing. Unlike local optimization, which can easily get stuck in local minima, simulated annealing allows rare \"uphill\" moves that enable exploration of diverse regions in the solution space. The key intuition is that the optimal solution might lie not just in the vicinity of the initial seed but requires a true global search. We'll frame this as an optimization over the midpoints between grid points, gradually refining the solution through random perturbations that respect both the integrality and box constraints. This approach is computationally more expensive than local minima but offers significantly better coverage across the solution manifold.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nWe consider h as defined on N=360 grid points, so the integral constraint \u222bh dx = 1 requires \u2211hdx = 1. We'll maintain a state vector s \u2208 \u211d^(360/2) where each s_i represents an interval midpoint: the function is 1 between consecutive midpoints and 0 elsewhere. The objective value C_5 for a given s is computed using:\n  raw_correlation = max(np.correlate(s, 1-s, mode='full'))\n  C_5 = raw_correlation * dx, where dx=2/360\n\nThe state space is constrained by:\n  0 \u2264 s[i] \u2264 dx for all i\n  \u2211s[i] = 1 / 2 (since \u222bh dx = \u2211(s[i] * 2 dx) = 1)\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Parent Validation:\n   - Input initial condition converted to midpoint midpoints with dx/2 random jitter to add diversity\n   - Ensure total length 1 by:\n        scale = (dx/2) / (np.sum(midpoints) * 2)\n        midpoints = midpoints * scale\n   - Clip to [0, dx/2] where dx=2/360\n\n2. Core Update Loop:\n   While timerunning:\n     For t in range(30000):\n       - Clone best_solution\n       - With probability 0.9-0.9999: Small perturbation (MSA: move a single endpoint \u00b1dx/2, MCH: probabilistic endpoint shift)\n       - With 0.0001-0.001 probability: Rare upward move (increase an interval at expense of others)\n       - Compute new_state = repair(new_state)\n       - Compute new_score = compute_C5(new_state)\n       - Apply simulated annealing: accept based on temperature schedule T(t) = 0.999^t\n\n3. Candidate Acceptance:\n   Track best_score and corresponding state. A move is accepted if:\n     new_score < best_score OR\n     exp((best_score - new_score)/T(t)) > uniform(0,1)\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Local transitions (MSA) always to prevent immediate collapse\n   - Use secondary random mapping (permutation with to migration behavior)\n   - Harbor temperature schedule balancing exploration and exploitation\n   - Multi-stage refinement from seed:\n\n5. Constraint Enforcement:\n   s[i] \u2208 [0, dx/2] for all i and \u2211s[i] = 1/2\n   Implemented as:\n       temp = current_state + random_step()\n       temp = np.clip(temp, 0, dx/2 - 1e-12)\n       For integrality: scale = (dx/2) / (np.sum(temp) * 2) + 1e-12\n           temp = temp * scale\n\n6. Validation Chain:\n   - Re-run fitness on final state after full cooling\n   - Check 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 by ensuring s[i]\u2208[0,dx/2]\n   - Correct integrality by post-calculation: desired_sum = dx/2 * 360/2 / dx\n                   current_sum = np.sum(s)\n                   scale_factor = desired_sum/current_sum + small_drift\n                   s = s * scale_factor\n\nThe annealing provides a mechanism to escape local trapping points while the simplex-inspired permutations allow exploration of unseen topologies. The fine-mesh initial condition (360 points) provides a good localized starting point but insufficient scope for optimal global structure discovery.\n\nSearch choices reflect the need to first generate diversity and then progressively refine based on objective value.\n\nComputational Budget:\n- Dominant cost: O(30000) function evaluations * O(N) correlations (360)\n- Estimated time: ~90 seconds per neighborhood iteration\n- Four-stage refinement with escalating temperature and decreasing perturbation rate.\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n1. Check initial fitness against record: if not improved immediately terminate\n2. Track raw C5 score with builtin dtype64 for precision\n3. Return h_values recalculated via endpoint positions and ensure sum(h)=1 with tolerance 1e-6\n4. Final validation: \n   - np.all(0<=h<=1)\n   - abs(sum(h)-n/2)*dx < 1e-6\n   - Compute_C5() using provided method\n\nThe new objective function is computed exactly as:\n  def compute_C5(h):\n      n=len(h)\n      dx=2.0/n\n      if not (np.all(h >= 0) and np.all(h<=1)):\n          h = np.clip(h,0,1)\n      integral = np.sum(h)*dx\n      # For record: h must be clamped and integrated exactly\n      corr = np.correlate(h,1.0-h,mode='full')\n      return float(np.max(corr)*dx)\n\nThis global algorithm may also incorporate a fallback to binary search in promising regions when temperature falls below 0.1, further enhancing exploration.",
